{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 5, :comments ["<b>And shines</b>. The scholars of measurement [astronomy] and calculation have already explained that all created visible things are upon ten parts, and the sun is greater than all of them, and none is like it. It is the root in the work [astronomy] of the heavens, like the point in the science of measurement and the alef in the science of calculation. The meaning: even though it is in motion, and shines and comes, it returns to its place as at first. The place of its rising today is close to the place of its rising tomorrow. Because of its movement to the north and to the south, it shines twice a year from one place, and from the place of the end of the south and the north once, until a full year is completed. And so the matter of the moon and the five planets, and all the host of the heavens—they shine and come. Therefore he mentioned the great one, as in the matter of 'for the sun, He set a tent in them' (Psalms 19:5)—for he did not mention the stars." "<b>And the meaning of 'panting'</b>. Like 'her soul comes, panting'—as if it pants after the wind from the abundance of its desire to return to its place."], :book :ecclesiastes, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 1, :notes {0 "Resolved החמשה as 'the five' (planets); the text reads והחשבון once but the parallel phrase בחכמת החשבון appears to be implied by context."}, :source-witnesses [["Wikisource" "https://he.wikisource.org/wiki/%D7%90%D7%91%D7%9F_%D7%A2%D7%96%D7%A8%D7%90_%D7%A2%D7%9C_%D7%A7%D7%94%D7%9C%D7%AA"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:26:57.307259314Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}